## Worked example — Mode C (one list segment, ~45 seconds)

> === SEG-02 · THE DARK · ~125 words ===
>
> You switch off the light, and for half a second the room stops being a room.
> [SEE: hand hits a light switch, the bedroom drops into darkness]
>
> Your ancestor didn't have a bedroom. He had a fire, and a fire has an edge, and past the edge was everything that could see in the dark better than he could.
> [SEE: the shadow on the wall becomes an ancestor at the edge of a firelit circle; two eyes open in the black beyond]
>
> In two thousand eleven, a team led by the ecologist Craig Packer looked at hundreds of lion attacks on people in Tanzania. Most came in the evening. And they piled up in the days after a full moon, when the sun has set and the moon hasn't risen yet.
> [SEE: moon-phase bar filling; a tally of attacks stacking up just after the full moon] [TEXT: WORST NIGHTS: AFTER FULL MOON]
>
> ANCESTOR_1: So we stay by the fire.
> ANCESTOR_2: What if I need to pee?
> ANCESTOR_1: Then we'll miss you.
>
> Nothing in your bedroom is a lion. Your brain has not been told.
> [SEE: back in the bedroom; the alarm meter reads nearly as high now as it did then]

Annotations: opens on an ordinary modern moment · the ancestor arrives through the shadow, not through exposition · the study is named with year, lead researcher, what was looked at and what was found, with no invented numbers · the cutaway delivers attitude, not facts, and works in plain voices with no delivery notes · the return to "you" is a flat, funny verdict, and the meter gives the editor a recurring beat · every sentence suggests a picture without naming a style · the year is written the way it should be said. (Verify Packer et al. 2011, PLoS ONE, before use.)

## Worked example — Mode A

> Everybody knows about the Salem witch trials. Almost nobody knows that the whole thing got a push from a cake.
>
> In early 1692, two girls in the Parris household started having fits — contorting, screaming, complaining that something was biting them. The local doctor examined them and reached the obvious medical conclusion available to a professional in 1692.
>
> DOCTOR: Witches.
> PARRIS: Are you sure?
> DOCTOR: I've ruled out everything else.
> PARRIS: What else did you check?
> DOCTOR: Nothing.
>
> A neighbor decided to help. Her plan was to take the girls' urine, bake it into a cake, and feed the cake to a dog, because the dog would then somehow reveal the witch. This is a real thing that a real adult did. The dog, to nobody's credit, declined to name anyone.
>
> But the cake made it official. Before the cake, two kids were sick. After the cake, there was a witch in the village, and the only remaining question was which of your neighbors it was. By autumn, nineteen people had been hanged, and one man had been slowly crushed under stones, which wasn't technically an execution, because he refused to enter a plea and the court had a procedure for that.
>
> He was somewhere around eighty.

Annotations: hook demolishes a known story · setup line makes the cutaway inevitable · cutaway is five lines and exits hard · specificity (urine, cake, dog) does the comedy · "to nobody's credit" is the one aside · escalation from farce to nineteen hangings · the ending is a fact delivered flat — and hedged, because his exact age is disputed.

## Worked example — Mode B

> You hatch into total darkness two thousand feet down, roughly the size of a grain of rice, and the situation is already worse than you know.
>
> The good news is you can swim. The bad news is everything else. There's no light down here, no plants, and whatever you eat has to fall from a world you'll never see. So you drift, you wait, and you get very good at doing nothing, because doing nothing is cheap.
>
> Then you grow up, and your body makes a decision on your behalf.
>
> Your digestive system starts to shut down. You are, from this point forward, no longer an animal that eats. What you are instead is a nose — an enormous, sensitive nose with a tail, one job, and a limited amount of stored energy to do it with.
>
> Somewhere out there in the black water is a female many times your size. You have to find her by smell, in the dark, before you starve.
>
> Let's say you do.

Annotations: cold open into the body · "already worse than you know" is a curiosity gap in six words · facts arrive as things happening to you · the escalation is a body failing on schedule · closes on a stall that opens the next section.

