## Workflow

1. State the premise in one sentence: what the video *shows*, not what it covers. Note the title and thumbnail the script must pay off.
2. Research. For each candidate fact, record the source and its evidence level (measured / well-supported / plausible story). Flag anything disputed or single-sourced.
3. Select the strongest facts: surprising, visual, escalatable, showable in a quick picture. Discard the merely true.
4. Pick the mode (Mode C for channel videos about the viewer).
5. Pick the spine and the ending shape together. For list videos, state the one rule and the recurring visual device.
6. Set the word budget: runtime × `NARRATOR_WPM`, split per segment.
7. Write the cold open to the channel template.
8. Build the beat map (function / information / payoff / open question). For list videos, order segments by escalating misfire and vary each segment's internal order.
9. Mark escalation points, cutaways (2–4, at most three character voices), callbacks, the health line if relevant, and tonal shifts, where the *material* suggests them.
10. Draft fast and conversationally in the draft format.
11. Read the whole thing aloud. Mark every place the voice stalls, flattens or sounds written.
12. **Cut.**
13. Rhythm and voice pass: sentence variation, ambiguous numbers written as spoken, no symbols, no delivery notes, nothing that only works with an emotion tag.
14. Curiosity check: list every gap opened and confirm each one is paid, including the cold-open promise.
15. Evidence check: every claim survives the joke, every named study verified or marked `[VERIFY]`, evidence levels honest.
16. Picture check: every stretch of about 2 seconds suggests something to show; nothing graphic; nothing style-dependent.
17. Rewrite weak sections. **Never repair by addition.**
18. Deliver the final script with the word budget table, sources, verify list, uncertainty note and pronunciation notes, then package it with the `video-production-format` skill.

## Quality control

Run all sixteen. Score each **pass / weak / fail**.

| Check | Question | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Hook** | Is the premise live within 15 seconds (~40 words)? Viewer's own moment first? | Rewrite to the cold-open template. |
| **First payoff** | Does the first named evidence land by ~2:00? | Move a study earlier; cut setup. |
| **Narrative** | Is this a story, or a list with jokes? Is there one rule tying the list together? | State the rule; re-map beats onto a spine. |
| **Retention** | Any stretch over ~15 s with no reward? | Cut it. |
| **Curiosity** | Gaps opened and paid every 30–60 s? Cold-open promise paid? | Add gaps at beat boundaries; pay the orphans. |
| **Humor** | Do jokes come from the material or get bolted on? | Delete bolted-on jokes. Find the joke inside the fact. |
| **Voice** | One calm, dry, specific narrator throughout? No hype, no gasping? | Flatten the delivery; replace intensifiers with facts. |
| **Rhythm** | Sounds good aloud? 4:1 length variation? | Break up uniform runs; add fragments. |
| **Escalation** | Is the last third the most intense or most absurd? | Reorder segments; hold back your best material. |
| **Template fatigue** | Do consecutive segments open or run the same way? | Rotate the segment template. |
| **Specificity** | Concrete details you'd remember tomorrow? | Replace intensifiers with objects and measurements. |
| **Evidence** | Every segment has a named, verified (or flagged) source? Evidence levels honest? No evo-psych just-so stories stated as fact? | Verify, hedge, or cut. |
| **Promise & safety** | Title and thumbnail paid off? Health line present once, sincere? No treatment advice? Nothing graphic? | Add the admission, the line, or swap the imagery. |
| **Pictures** | Does every ~2 s suggest something to show? Text card every 15–30 s? Nothing graphic or style-dependent? | Convert abstractions into things; cut style words. |
| **Voice & length** | Ambiguous numbers written as spoken, no symbols, no delivery notes, every character line works in a plain voice, ≤ 3 character voices, within 10% of word target? | Fix the lines; rewrite attitude into the words; cut to length. |
| **AI detection / Ending** | Anything sterile, even or polished? Is the last line a payoff, not a summary? | Run the diagnostic and repair drill; pick a shape from Step 10. |

**One fail, or three weaks, means rewriting the affected sections — not polishing them.**

Run the AI-detection check last, and run it adversarially: argue that your own script was machine-written, gather the evidence, then destroy the evidence.
